 movies we know and love  today  wow  roger ebert would add seven samurai to  his coveted list of great movies it was  ranked number three on the guardians  poll of the 40 greatest foreign films of  all time and timeout magazine which i'm  told is not time magazine voted the  second best action film ever welcome to  the club seven samurai  rebecca  no stop  what the level you're shouting about  [Music]  who are you  what are you staring at  i'm sorry i didn't mean to stare but i i  only thought oh you did it too well what  are you doing here  i was only walking  well get on with your walking don't hang  about here screaming what would a list  of silver screen perfection be without  some hitchcock specifically 1940s  rebecca the director's first american  project the film is based on the daphne  de maurier novel of the same name and  features performances by lawrence  olivier and joan fontaine it also earned  producer david o selznick his second  best picture oscar in a row after  winning for gone with the win the  previous year kind of hard to believe  but this is the only hitchcock film ever  to take home a best picture oscar  despite several of his films being  nominated in that category and when  psycho is nominated you should have seen  the dress that mrs bates wore rebecca is  the story of a young woman who marries a  mysterious widower named max dewinter  and soon finds herself living in the  shadow of his former wife who  suspiciously died years earlier it  sounds like your average hitchcockian  tale so what sets this film apart from  any other work by the master of suspense  well the guardian called it one of  hitchcock's creepiest most oppressive  films which is really saying something  other critics wrote that it's subtle  electrifying and almost as fascinating  as the novel and that hitchcock  succeeded brilliantly in his initial  hollywood venture adding that he has  retold in his own creepy colorful way mr  marie's forbidding fascinating story of  life 