n you wish upon a star and  critics have called it emotionally  resonant with animation that is nothing  short of masterful  [Music]  me too father  [Music]  over 80 years later these characters and  themes continue to inspire welcome to  the club pinocchio 12 angry men say  something  you lousy bunch of bleeding hearts  you're not gonna intimidate me i'm  entitled to my opinion legendary  filmmaker sydney lumet directed 12 angry  men a courtroom drama released in 1957.  it was written by reginald rose and  produced by henry fonda who also starred  along with lee cobb ed begley e.g  marshall and jack warden the film  follows the closing arguments in a  murder trial where the fate of one inner  city teenager rests in the hands of the  12-member jury a guilty verdict means  the death penalty and much conflict  unfolds in the jury's deliberation hence  the movie's title the film was  originally produced as a television play  and after a successful run on the  program studio one rose adapted to  teleplay for the big screen the story's  inspiration came from rose's real life  experience as a jury member on a  manslaughter case according to rose  there was a terrific furious eight-hour  argument in the jury room and he  instantly realized this was the perfect  setting for a drama once the screenplay  was complete lumet was recruited and  this became his first feature film as a  director aside from rose's clever  writing and lamech sharp direction henry  fonda's performance as juror 8 is  considered by many to be his career best  although the jury's still out on that  one considering fonda has 11 films rated  100 percent that's got to be a record  right twelve angry men was revolutionary  for exploring issues of race and social  class and how those issues influenced  the justice system the film also ignited  the courtroom procedural craze that  still sweeps the screen today seen in  movies like the trial to chicago 7 and  shows like juan order bum bum the los  angeles times called 12 angry men a tour  de force in movie making the new york  times wrote that it makes for a taut  absorbing and compelling drama that  reaches far beyond the close confines of  its jury room setting secret what do you  mean secret there are no secrets in a  jewelry room i know who it was  brother you really are something you sit  here vote guilty like the rest of us and  some golden voice preachers starts  tearing your poor heart out about some  underprivileged kid just couldn't help  becoming a murderer and you change your  vote  if that isn't the most sick why don't  you drop a quarter in this collection  box and timeout wrote that lumet's  window into strained civic duty will  continue to serve mightily and that's  the whole truth your honor cool hand  luke what we've got here is  failure to communicate  some man  you just can't reach  so you get what we had here last week  which is the way he wants it  well he gets it cool hand luke was  released in october of 1967 and is based  on don pierce's novel of the same name  paul newman plays luke jackson a petty  criminal who gets a two-year sentence in  a florida prison and refuses to conform  to the shady antics of the warden or to  the established pecking order amongst  the inmates luke's behaviors lead him  into some painful situations such as  solitary confinement and imbalanced fist  fights and his numerous attempts to  escape only serve to escalate his  suffering but luke doesn't stay down and  soon he earns the respect of the other  prisoners and takes on the role of a  system-fighting martyr newman's  performance is one for the ages one that  no other actor could have pulled off  with such charisma and physical  determination interestingly enough  newman almost didn't get the part as  jack lemmon was originally selected to  portray luke but after lemon read the  script he believed that newman would be  a better fit so he passed and chose to  be a producer on the film instead this  might be the only live action movie on  our list where the director's talents  are overlooked though stuart rosenberg  had plenty of success and frequently  collaborated with newman many felt that  his direction on this film didn't cut it  the chicago reader even called  rosenberg's work a horror  yikes maybe the guy just had a failure  to communicate even with that cool han  luke received a near flawless critical  reception i'm gonna eat 50 eggs  nobody can eat 58. you just said he  could eat anything w58  nobody ever eat 50 eggs hey my blue guts  we got a bet here  my boy says he can eat 50 eggs he can  eat 50 eggs yeah man how long  that hour the film also earned four  oscar nominations grossed over 16  million dollars on a three million  dollar budget and forever cemented  newman as a cultural icon man on wire  what i do is under the copy of sneeze ah  i see the page put it under my jacket  and go out now of course i would have a  toothache for a week but what's the pain  in comparison that now i have acquired  my dream  man on wire is a biographical  documentary that chronicles daredevil  philly petit's high wire walk across the  world trade center buildings in 1974  director james marsh blends actual  footage with reenactments and uses  present day interviews to gain further  insight into the infamous stunt the film  is based on petite's 2002 book the  recounts his experience and the title  refers to the words written on the  police report that led to his arrest  petite is one of those characters that  seems too bizarre to be real one that  even the most astute of screenwriters  couldn't possibly have created he oozes  with personality superhuman skill and  speaks with such an eloquence and charm  rivaled only by that of your host today  not really he has this unwavering  passion for high wire walking it  consumes him and we the audience can't  help but be inspired by his conviction  not so much inspire that we're going to  walk across a rope that's staying with  1300 feet in the air but it gets you  amped up it's no wonder that joseph  gordon-levitt chose to portray petite in  the 2015 film the walk which is robert  domecca's dramatic take on the same  event man on wire took home the oscar  for best documentary feature at the 81st  academy awards and additionally won  sundance's grand jury prize for the  world cinema documentary grabbed me and  grounded me handcuffed actually hands in  the back i was thrown down the staircase  the most dangerous part of this whole  adventure i almost break my head on the  narrow staircase that lead to the  elevator the washington post wrote to  the film is an absorbing mischievously  amusing yarn whose climax unfolds with  unexpected emotional force and other  critics called it exhilarating and palm  poisoning leave no trace so what are you  doing here  i wasn't where i was supposed to be so  they took me away  oh  they don't think i was where i was  supposed to be last up is writer  director deborah granick's 2018 feature  leave no trace which premiered at  sundance and was distributed by bleeker  street media ben foster and rising star  and mackenzie brilliantly portray will  and tom a father and daughter living on  a nature reserve outside of portland  oregon the film is based on the true  story told in peter rock's novel my  abandonment published in 2009 and the  title refers to the leave no trace set  of ethics that began in the 1960s and is  used to promote conservation in the  outdoors granic explores socio-economic  environmental and mental health issues  in a way that is subtle yet impactful  and worthy of much praise the los  angeles times called leave no trace  fiercely evolving in a way we're not  used to made with sensitivity and  honesty and the guardian called it a  deeply intelligent story of love and  survival in the wild  so you don't need to be scared  additionally the film is nominated for  three satellite awards and two gotham  awards there you have it our selections  from the rotten 